<p>Many property claims are estimated from the outside, before anyone pulls off a shingle or opens a wall. Then the work starts and the real scope shows up: soft decking, wet insulation, a cracked rafter, or a code requirement the first estimate did not include. <strong>Insurance claim supplements in San Marcos</strong> are how those additional items are documented and presented to the insurer. As a San Marcos public adjuster, we help property owners identify what was missed, tie each item to the original loss, and submit it in a form the insurer can review.</p>

<h2>What a Supplement Can Cover</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Supplement type</th><th>Example</th><th>Documentation that helps</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hidden damage</td><td>Water-damaged decking under hail-damaged shingles; wet framing behind siding</td><td>Photos during tear-off before the area is closed up, with measurements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Missed items</td><td>Gutters, vents, flashing, screens, fences, or interior ceilings not in the estimate</td><td>Photos of each item and its location on a diagram</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quantity corrections</td><td>Roof squares, waste factor, ridge or eave lengths measured short</td><td>Independent measurements or a roof report</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code-required work</td><td>Items required by current codes when repairing covered damage</td><td>Permit records, code citations, and ordinance or law coverage review</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pricing adjustments</td><td>Specialty materials or trades priced below local cost</td><td>Itemized local invoices and supplier quotes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional living or business costs</td><td>Longer displacement or shutdown than first estimated</td><td>Receipts, leases, and a revised timeline</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Why Supplements Are Common in San Marcos Claims</h2>
<p>Hays County roofs see a lot of spring weather; NOAA records 180 hail reports in the county since 1967, peaking in April and May. Large events like <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181388" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the May 9, 2024 storm</a>, which dropped 3-inch hail west of San Marcos, send many adjusters into the field at once, and quick first inspections can miss items that only show up later.</p>
<p>Construction details matter too. Under the 2021 codes the City of San Marcos has enforced since April 1, 2023, some repairs require upgrades that a basic replace-in-kind estimate leaves out. The city's local amendment means a roof covering replacement does not need a permit unless the new roof is heavier than the existing one, but other work, such as structural or interior repairs, may need permits. Whether code costs are payable depends on your ordinance or law coverage. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page summarizes the details, and nearby cities like Kyle and Buda have their own permit offices.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/supplement-process-collage.webp" alt="Collage showing a roof inspection, phone photos, estimating software, and a handshake" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A supplement links new findings to the original claim through photos, measurements, and an updated estimate.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>The Supplement Process, Step by Step</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop and document.</strong> When hidden damage appears, photograph it in place, measure it, and note its location before it is repaired or covered.</li>
<li><strong>Notify the insurer.</strong> Tell the adjuster promptly and offer an opportunity to re-inspect. Many policies have notice and inspection conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare the request.</strong> Each added item should appear as a line item, with quantity, price, and the photo or measurement that supports it. We prepare these in Xactimate so they line up with the insurer's estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Explain the connection.</strong> Show how each item relates to the covered loss. Rotted decking from years of slow leaks is a different matter from decking soaked through new hail damage.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up in writing.</strong> Track the response, answer questions, and respond to any items the insurer declines.</li>
</ol>

